cisions to controlling the financial viability of independent verification systems. When public good provision depends on private platform funding, those platforms can eliminate entire accountability ecosystems through single policy reversals. The pattern suggests need for public funding or platform-independent fact-checking infrastructure.\",\n        \"confidence\": 0.85\n      }\n    ],\n    \"omissions\": [\n      {\n        \"what_is_missing\": \"No published comparative pilot data showing Community Notes would outperform professional fact-checking on Meta's specific platforms and user populations\",\n        \"why_it_matters\": \"Evidence-based policy would test alternatives before global rollout. The absence suggests the decision wasn't evidence-based but rather politically motivated with post-hoc rationalization.\",\n        \"possible_explanations\": [\n          \"No pilots were conducted because decision was political not empirical\",\n          \"Pilots showed Community Notes performed worse but results were suppressed\",\n          \"Meta leadership didn't consider evidence necessary for politically expedient decision\"\n        ]\n      },\n      {\n        \"what_is_missing\": \"Detailed data on systematic political bias in Meta's fact-checking program that would justify the 'too politically biased' claim\",\n        \"why_it_matters\": \"The stated justification for termination lacks supporting evidence. If bias existed, Meta should publish data; absence suggests the bias claim is pretextual.\",\n        \"possible_explanations\": [\n          \"No systematic bias existed—claim is false\",\n          \"Any bias was correctable through better partner selection rather than program elimination\",\n          \"Meta adopted conservative talking points without internal evidence base\"\n        ]\n      },\n      {\n        \"what_is_missing\": \"Discussion of international regulatory compliance (EU DSA, Brazil, Australia) in Meta's public justification for